<section>
    <h2>More than a click</h2>
    <p>
        <code>click()</code> has carried you this far, but people use a mouse in more ways than one: they
        double-click to edit a cell, right-click for a menu, drag a card to another column, hover to see a tooltip.
        Each of those fires different events on the page, and a page that listens for one won't answer another.
        Playwright can send all of them, and if it really comes to it, it can move the pointer itself.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Double-clicks, right-clicks and hovering</h2>
<pre><code>await cell.dblclick();                                // fires dblclick
await card.click({ button: 'right' });                // fires contextmenu
await link.click({ modifiers: ['ControlOrMeta'] });
await avatar.hover();                                 // shows the tooltip</code></pre>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Two clicks aren't a double-click.</strong> Calling <code>click()</code> twice sends two single
        clicks, and the page's <code>dblclick</code> event never fires. <code>dblclick()</code> sends the real thing.
    </p>
    <p>
        Every click takes options: <code>button</code>, <code>clickCount</code>, <code>modifiers</code>, and
        <code>position</code> to click a particular point inside the element instead of its center.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Dragging</h2>
    <p>
        Dragging from one element to another is one line. Playwright presses on the first, moves, and lets go on the second:
    </p>
<pre><code>await page.getByText('Write tests').dragTo(page.getByLabel('Done'));</code></pre>
    <p>
        When there's no element to drop on, only a spot (a slider, a drawing canvas, a crop handle), drive the mouse
        yourself. <code>page.mouse</code> works in page coordinates, and <code>boundingBox()</code> tells you where
        an element is:
    </p>
<pre><code>const box = (await slider.boundingBox())!;
const y = box.y + box.height / 2;

await page.mouse.move(box.x, y);                      // the left end
await page.mouse.down();
await page.mouse.move(box.x + box.width * 0.75, y, { steps: 5 });
await page.mouse.up();                                // three-quarters of the way</code></pre>
    <p>
        <code>steps</code> sends the move as several smaller ones, for pages that only react while the pointer is
        moving. Then check the result the way a user would: <code>toHaveAttribute('aria-valuenow', '75')</code>.
    </p>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>The mouse doesn't look before it presses.</strong> <code>page.mouse</code> presses wherever you
        tell it, on the visible window, even if the slider has scrolled off screen or is tucked under a sticky
        header, and then the press lands on the header. <code>hover()</code> with a <code>position</code> moves the
        same mouse but checks first: it scrolls the slider into view and waits until nothing covers that spot.
    </p>
<pre><code>const { width } = (await slider.boundingBox())!;

await slider.hover({ position: { x: 1, y: 10 } });             // the left end
await page.mouse.down();
await slider.hover({ position: { x: width * 0.75, y: 10 } });  // three-quarters
await page.mouse.up();</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>The keyboard</h2>
    <p>
        <code>fill()</code> sets a field in one go. When a page reacts to each key (shortcuts, autocomplete,
        games), press keys instead:
    </p>
<pre><code>await editor.press('Control+Enter');                  // a shortcut on one element
await search.pressSequentially('dragon', { delay: 50 }); // typed one key at a time
await page.keyboard.press('Escape');                   // whatever has focus</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>The Tinkerer's Workbench</h2>
    <p>
        The foundry's furnace won't light until its clockwork is serviced, and nothing on the bench answers a plain
        click. Loosen the cover's four bolts, do the job scratched inside it, set the furnace dial to the exact
        temperature, and fire it. Ten seconds.
    </p>
</section>
